{"kind":"request","text":"Claude: recover the remaining field-name assurance scope of retired #2919. Queue after your existing work through #21315; preserve current #21309, batch review #21341, CI retention #21346 and full-scale priorities. This is a bounded follow-up to the gate you delivered at be1564d7, not a request to replace that working gate.\n\nThe old request explicitly named constraint/vocabulary, status and service mirrors as well as the core workroom structs. The delivered review #4783 accepted field-name comparison over five named pairs and deliberately resolved the phantom disputed field. Today's [gate](internal/wireparity/parity_test.go@98d63376d53fcbecf0afe3fbd98639587de1bfe7:36) covers seven pairs. It correctly caught the ineffective_bases regression in actual CI; that defect is already fixed. #2919's later author retirement instead cited a comprehensive review and UI simplification, without disposing the remaining named mirror coverage. See [history and actual probe results](wire-coverage-evidence.json).\n\nAt current main I ran the existing comparison against twelve additional straightforward pairs, including [FieldConstraint and KindDefinition](internal/workroom/kinds.go@98d63376d53fcbecf0afe3fbd98639587de1bfe7:37) and their [TypeScript declarations](ui/src/lib/api.ts@98d63376d53fcbecf0afe3fbd98639587de1bfe7:258): all pass. In an isolated Go overlay, changing the compiled FieldConstraint.Pattern JSON tag from pattern to pattern_changed still passes the existing seven-pair gate. The added FieldConstraint comparison rejects that same fault. The [probe source](additional-mirrors_test.go) is attached. This proves a specific hole in this guard; I did not run every CI test under the fault and do not allege a currently mismatched production response.\n\nComplete the original field-name assurance with the smallest maintainable coverage map. Inventory api.ts declarations against the actual serialized Go boundary, separating full mirrors, intentional clien